## Why the redesign

The M1 greybox worked, but it asked too much of Ben. The board alone had about 14 stops, a
cheeseburger took about 29 selects, and a two-burger order measured 54 selects and 150
Space moves. The player should not have to go looking for each ingredient: **the
ingredients come to him.** He still has to remember and follow the order, and he can still
get it wrong, but he never has to navigate to find anything.

## What the player sees

**Main menu:** Start Shift · Tutorial · Settings · Exit Game. My Diner and Free Cook join it
in M6.

### An order, step by step

1. **Order.** A customer walks up to the counter and says the order out loud, the way people
   order: "Evening! Bacon cheeseburger, medium, no onions." Then it is over to the kitchen,
   where the ticket comes round the ticket wheel and the game reads it once in its own voice.
   **The ticket spells out the whole order** (see [The ticket](#the-ticket)): "Ticket. Bacon
   Cheeseburger: one patty, medium, with cheese, bacon, lettuce, tomato and ketchup." So a
   customer's accent, a "the usual, hon" or a "no onions" never hides what goes on, and
   nobody has to know what a menu item comes with. It stays in view in the kitchen for the
   whole order.
2. **Grill.** The game offers patties one at a time: add a patty, add a second patty, and so
   on. Then it asks how to cook them. The patties cook, flip by themselves and come off.
3. **Board.** The burger is started for the player (bottom bun, then the patties). Each
   ingredient then comes down the line one by one, and the player picks Add or Skip for
   each. The top bun goes on by itself.
4. **Review.** The finished order sits on the pass, and the game says what was made, in
   plain words. The player can serve it, or go back and redo the grill or the toppings.
5. **Serve.** The player rings it up at the cash register. The customer reacts, the order is
   rated, the customer says exactly what was wrong if anything was (up to two things, then
   "and that is not even everything"), and a receipt shows the stars, every mistake in words
   and the tip. Its choices: the counter bell (Next Customer), Hear It Again, Pause.

Orders with more than one item run the line once per item, in the ticket's order, then one
review and one serve for the whole order.

A shift is a set number of customers (Shift Length). Then a results card shows the stars
and tips, and the player can start another shift or go to the main menu.

### The ticket

Decided 2026-11-07 after the first playtest: the ticket said "no pickles, no ketchup", the
player put everything else on, and the customer got mad, because a cheeseburger as written
has no onion, mustard or mayo, and nothing said so. So the ticket now **lists everything**:
for each item its name, how many patties and how done, then everything that goes on, in the
order the line brings it ("Cheeseburger: one patty, medium, with cheese, lettuce, tomato,
pickles and ketchup"; "nothing else on it" when there is nothing). The rule is always the
same and needs no menu knowledge: **add what's on the ticket, skip what isn't.** With more
than one item, the item being made is spelt out on the wheel and the others wait as their
names; the read-out ("Ticket. ...") says every item in full. The customer still orders the
natural way ("no onions"), in their own recorded voice; the ticket is the one to follow.
`words.js` builds both (`said()` for the customer, `ticket()`/`item()` for the ticket), and a
test checks over hundreds of random orders that the ticket lists exactly what the line wants.

### Mistakes

The player can still make mistakes. If the ticket has no cheese and Ben adds cheese, the
customer is annoyed. Nothing before the serve says something is wrong (except in the
tutorial), and every mistake is named afterwards in the customer's review.

### Choice limits

Every screen has the blank -1 and Pause, plus no more than five choices, even on the most
complex step. The phases are there to keep it that way. Ticket is one of the five, so a step
offers at most four answers; a step with more (six shake flavours, one day) shows three and
a "More…" row.

### Writing tone

The customers are funny and a bit absurd, written for adults (ACCESSIBILITY §1). No baby
talk, no "great job!" after every press, no talking down. The joke lands on the customer,
never on the player, and nothing ever comments on the player's speed or skill.

## The assembly line

### Phases

Each recipe names the stations it goes through, and each station is a **phase**: a short
list of steps. Every step is one of three kinds, and these are all the line needs:

| Step | What the player sees | Answers |
|---|---|---|
| **Add** | One ingredient, offered on its own. A step can offer the same thing again up to a limit ("Add a second patty?") | Add · Skip, plus Swap It when the ingredient is bad ([Gross-outs](#gross-outs)) |
| **Cook** | The food on the heat | Simple: the doneness options. Timed: Take It Off |
| **Pick** | One choice of a few: fries or onion rings, a cup size, a soda | The options, up to five |

Every screen also has **Ticket** (in the kitchen it turns the ticket wheel once round and says
the order again; at the counter it is the order's own slip on the panel; the highlight stays
on it) and **Pause** (always last).

| Station | Steps, in order (first release in bold) |
|---|---|
| **Grill** | **Add patty (up to 3) → Cook** |
| **Board** | **Bottom bun and patties go on by themselves → Add or Skip: cheese, bacon, lettuce, tomato, pickles, onion, ketchup, mustard, mayo → top bun by itself** |
| Fryer | Pick fries or onion rings → Cook → Add salt |
| Drinks | Pick the cup size → Add ice → Pick the drink |
| Shake Bar | Pick the flavour → Pick 1, 2 or 3 scoops → Add whipped cream, cherry, sprinkles (it blends by itself) |
| Griddle | Pancakes and grilled cheese, as Add and Cook steps (M5) |

- **The stack order is the line's order,** so it is never something to get wrong.
- **Sauces are Add or Skip.** The amount is always a normal squeeze, so tickets no longer ask
  for "a dab" or "a line". A sauce comes down the line in its squeeze bottle (red ketchup,
  yellow mustard, white mayo); Add turns the bottle over above the burger and squirts a
  zigzag line across it (about a second and a half, with a soft squirt sound; Motion Reduced
  just puts the sauce on). Skip slides the bottle on down the line.
- **Bacon is a board ingredient** (pre-cooked, from the warmer), so the grill phase is
  patties only. This gives the "overcooked food" gross-out its home: charred bacon.
- **After a choice, the highlight stays on the same answer** as the next ingredient arrives
  (the label changes, "Add lettuce" → "Add tomato", and is spoken). Three Adds in a row are
  three presses. A new phase opens fresh on the blank -1.

### Review and fixing

The review screen shows the order on the pass beside the ticket. It says what was made, in
plain words ("Cheeseburger: patty medium, cheese, lettuce, tomato, ketchup"). That is a
description, not a verdict: comparing it with the ticket is the player's job.

Its choices: **Serve** (ring it up) · **Redo the Grill** · **Redo the Toppings** · Ticket ·
Pause. With more than one item, Redo… is a row of the items, each with its phases. A redo
runs that phase again from its first step, and the review comes back after it.

### Cooking

Flipping is no longer a choice: it happens by itself, as part of the animation.

- **Simple** (default). The player picks a doneness: Rare, Medium, Medium Well or Well Done.
  The patty cooks to exactly that, with a short animation, an automatic flip and a sizzle.
  It always comes out as chosen.
- **Timed** (challenge). The patty cooks through Raw → Rare → Medium → Medium Well → Well Done
  → Charred while the player watches, and they press Take It Off at the right moment. This
  is the one place the cooking can go wrong. The timing is forgiving:
  - **Cooking Speed** (Very Slow by default) sets the stage length; the stage the ticket
    asks for lasts twice as long, so there is a wide window.
  - Every stage change is shown on the grill's doneness gauge (a word and a mark), changes
    the sizzle, and its name is spoken. Nothing depends on colour or on sight alone.
  - Mitch gives a heads-up one stage early ("Nearly medium."), under his speaking rules.
  - The clock never moves the highlight or changes the choices, only the labels. It freezes
    whenever Pause, Settings or a card is open, or the window loses focus. There is no
    deadline: food left on stops at Charred, which costs one mistake.

Timed mode is a choice scan over a slowly changing world, as Ari approved on 2026-11-01:
Take It Off is selected like any other choice, on release, so a held switch is fine.

## Rating

The rating is based on accuracy, never on time, and how to earn three stars is obvious:

- **3 stars:** everything matches the ticket.
- **2 stars:** one or two mistakes.
- **1 star:** more than that. There is no zero and no fail.

One mistake is any one of these: a topping or sauce added that is not on the ticket, or
skipped that is; one patty too many or too few; the wrong doneness; a wrong pick (size,
drink, fries for onion rings); a bad ingredient served ([Gross-outs](#gross-outs)).

The customer's review says exactly what was wrong, in words and in their own voice, so the
player knows what to fix next time. An extra topping is said two ways: "I said no cheese!"
only when the customer did say no to it, and "I didn't ask for onions." when it simply was not
on the ticket (`complain.unasked`; the tutorial's warning splits the same way). A receipt slip
shows the same list in words with the stars and the tip. With no mistakes, the customer just
enjoys it.

**Tips:** price × (0.6 + 0.2 × stars), at least 1 coin. A customer's favourite (Dale's extra
crispy rings) adds a bonus tip and a happy line, and never changes the stars, so the stars
stay purely about following the ticket.

## Tutorial

An optional tutorial walks the player through one full, typical order: the customer's
order, the grill, the board, the review and the serve. It is on the main menu, and the
first Start Shift on a new save offers it (Play the Tutorial · Skip It).

During the tutorial only, the game warns before a mistake lands. The choice is not applied;
Mitch says what is wrong ("Hold on, they said no onions. That's not the right one.") and
the step asks again. Outside the tutorial there are no warnings like this, and mistakes show
up in the customer's review.

The tutorial order includes one gross-out (green cheese), so the player meets Swap It once
with Mitch there to explain it.

## Gross-outs

Now and then something is wrong with an ingredient: a hair in it, a fly on it, mouldy
cheese, bad lettuce, or overcooked (charred) bacon. That's one or two per shift at most,
never two on one order, and only ever on an Add step.

The player has to be able to catch it, so it is flagged three ways:

- **Visually:** stink lines off the cheese, the cheese turning green, a fly circling, a hair
  across the tomato, smoke off the bacon.
- **Mitch's voiceover:** "Whoa, why is that cheese green? You nasty. Change that out."
- **The spoken label names it:** "Add the green cheese". This is the call's two flags plus
  one, because ACCESSIBILITY §9 does not allow a cue that needs sight, and Mitch can be
  switched off.

That step gains a third answer, **Swap It**, which brings a fresh one and asks again.
Catching it keeps the order clean. Missing it counts as one mistake and gets a funny,
disgusted reaction from the customer.

## Customers and cast

The cast list and each character's personality are still open (see [Open
questions](#open-questions)). The working cast is the one drafted on 2026-11-01, so that
art and voices can start:

| Customer | Who they are | Voice | Usually orders | Quirk |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Marge | Retired school-bus driver, cardigan, reading glasses on a chain | Warm, unhurried | "The usual, hon": cheeseburger, medium, iced tea | Says "the usual" and expects the player to know. The ticket shows it anyway |
| Dale | Wiry mechanic in grease-spotted overalls | Gravelly drawl | Onion rings, extra crispy, with anything | The onion-ring guy |
| Priya | Night-shift nurse in scrubs, coffee-deprived | Dry, quick | Pancakes at any hour | Orders breakfast at dinner time and refuses to discuss it |
| Coach Bev | Whistle, tracksuit, clipboard | Low, butch and booming (voice test, 2026-11-08) | Double bacon cheeseburger | Counts the pickles out loud on serve |
| Professor Oakes | Tweed jacket, bow tie, pocket protector | Nasal, nerdy, slightly too fast | Grilled cheese, lemonade | Hates onions, with a new over-researched reason each visit |
| Gus | Long-haul trucker, huge beard, trucker cap | Deep and friendly, with a Western drawl, not slow (voice test) | Two of one thing: "One for now, one for the road." | Big tips. Never in a hurry |
| Reginald | Self-declared food critic, scarf in all weather | Theatrical, sweeping | Whatever is newest on the menu | Reviews every order like a five-course meal |
| Tasha | Teenage skateboarder, deeply unimpressed | Flat, deadpan | Milkshakes | Pretends not to care, cheers quietly on a 3-star shake |
| Kai | Ten years old, notebook under one arm, Reginald's biggest fan | Bright, very serious | A small burger and a small drink | Copies Reginald's reviews but gets to the point |
| Vernon | Runs a burger-review blog with eleven followers; arms permanently folded | Foppish, fussy and refined, with theatrical sighs (voice test: was deep and droning) | Very exact orders: "Cheeseburger. Medium. No pickles. I will know." | Picky about the food, never the player. 1 star: "I've had warmer welcomes from a vending machine." 3 stars, after a long pause: "…Acceptable." |
| Sal | Runs the hot-dog cart out front and never eats his own hot dogs: flat cap, walrus moustache, mustard-stained apron, coin changer (added 2026-11-08) | Gruff, loud New Yorker, a bit crude | Bacon cheeseburgers (his favourite), doubles, now and then the Tower; **never lettuce** | Every order leaves off the lettuce, then: "Lettuce is for rabbits. Do I look like a rabbit?" The crude jokes land on him, his hot dogs and the food |

Rules that still hold for every customer:

- **Order size follows the Orders setting,** whoever is ordering. A personality changes what
  they order, never how many presses it takes.
- **The ticket always shows the whole order,** in pictures and words.
- **No customer brings a new mechanic,** changes their mind, leaves or hurries the player.
- **Snark aims at the food, never at the player.**

**First art and voice pass (proposed):** Mitch, Marge, Gus, Coach Bev and Vernon. All four
customers order burgers, and their voices are as different as the cast gets. **Sal** joined
them on 2026-11-08 (the user asked for a gruff, crude New Yorker; an original character, not a
copy of any show's): ten poses, his own lines, and a line when an order leaves off something
a customer never takes (`order.dislike`, the moment Professor Oakes's onion reasons will use).

**Walk-ins** (one-off customers built from parts, M5 in the old plan) do not fit whole-pose
expression images, which cannot be assembled from parts. They become a pool of extra drawn
customers later, each with its own images and voice (open question).

### Mitch, the assistant manager

Mitch narrates the diner, teaches the tutorial and spots gross-outs, in the same voice as
everything else: funny, but there to help. Short-sleeved shirt and tie, a headset he does
not need, and a name badge where "ASST." is a strip of tape over "MANAGER". He appears as a
portrait in a corner, never over a scan target.

| When | Example |
|---|---|
| A shift starts | "Doors are open. Try not to set anything on fire." |
| The tutorial, each phase | "This is the grill. One patty per burger, unless they say double." |
| The tutorial, before a mistake lands | "Hold on, they said no onions. That's not the right one." |
| A gross-out comes down the line | "Whoa, why is that cheese green? You nasty. Change that out." |
| Timed: one stage before the ticket's stage | "Nearly medium. That's what Marge wanted." |
| After a customer's reaction | Sometimes a one-liner, never instead of them |

**When he talks** (these protect the scan, ACCESSIBILITY §4):

- **Scan speech always wins.** He never cuts off a scan label and never holds the scanner's
  speech ticket.
- **He speaks into silence.** Parked time is his time; in two-switch play he speaks between
  presses. If no silence comes within a few seconds, a heads-up becomes a caption and a
  sound cue. A line that is no longer true by then is dropped. A gross-out line is the
  exception: it is a step's own announcement, so it is said when the bad ingredient arrives,
  before the step's label.
- **Priority:** gross-outs and heads-ups, then tutorial lines, then jokes. At most one joke
  per customer, and no line repeats in a shift.
- **Silent** while Pause, Settings or a card is open.
- **Never comments on how long anything took,** or on the player's speed or skill.

The **Assistant Manager** setting: Hints and Jokes (default) / Hints Only / Off. Hints Only
keeps gross-out lines and heads-ups. Off silences him; the gross-out still shows and its
label still names it.

## Look and feel: 2.5D

- **3D (Three.js): the food, the ingredients and the cooking.** Chunky, cel-shaded food in
  close-up: patties sizzle and flip, cheese drapes, toppings drop onto the stack.
- **2D characters:** cartoony and cel-shaded, in the spirit of SpongeBob and Bob's Burgers,
  but all original designs. No animation. Each character has about ten static expression
  images, each a **whole pose**, body and arms included (hands thrown up when angry), with
  its own clear silhouette so the reaction reads from the outline alone. When the emotion
  changes, the new pose pops in, with a quick squash-and-bounce on Full motion and a plain
  cut on Reduced (proposal; bounce or cut is open). The camera keeps the character big
  (about half the screen height at the counter), large enough for a player with low vision
  to see who they are and how they are reacting.
- **Backdrops:** each station has a static 2D cartoon backdrop that fits where it is in the
  restaurant. At the counter, a line of waiting customers stands behind the current one
  (sharp like the rest of the picture: the soft blur once put on them was taken out on
  2026-11-07), and behind them the doors, artwork, and the tables and chairs. The
  kitchen stations get backdrops that match them. Every backdrop hides a few funny little
  details for anyone who notices them.
- **Final menus** (title, settings, pause, results) are 2D diner boards (proposal; 2D or 3D
  is open).

### Expressions (proposal; the mapping is open)

| Customer pose | When |
|---|---|
| neutral | walks up, waits |
| talking | says the order, says a line |
| waiting | while the order is made (seen from the review) |
| happy | 3 stars |
| delighted | 3 stars and a favourite |
| annoyed | 2 stars |
| angry | 1 star ("I said no cheese!") |
| disgusted | a gross-out was served |
| shocked | a surprise (a charred patty) |
| laughing | a joke line |

Mitch's ten: neutral, talking, pointing (tutorial), thumbs up, shocked and disgusted
(gross-outs), facepalm, laughing, worried (timed heads-up), shrug.

### No floating UI

Wherever possible, the interface is a real object in the diner:

| Choice | Real object |
|---|---|
| Ticket | the order on the ticket wheel (kitchen); the order's slip on the panel (counter) |
| Add · Skip · Swap It | big enamel push-buttons on the line's control box, under the ingredient |
| Doneness | the plates on the grill's dial |
| Take It Off | the spatula hanging at the grill |
| Serve | the cash register: "Ring It Up" |
| Next Customer | the counter bell |
| Pause | the "Back in 5" sign on its hook |

Labels stay large and the highlight stays obvious (colour and thickness, never colour alone,
at least 64 px), so it is always clear what is selected. Doneness always shows as a word and
a mark (grill lines per stage), never colour alone.

**Where things sit** (settled by the M2 review frames): the food is low and left of centre on
the 3D work surface, which starts where the backdrop's painted surface does, so the wall and
its gags stay in view; the line's control panel is a column on the right; the "Back in 5"
sign is bottom left, where every backdrop is plain (the gags live in the top corners). The
scan therefore runs answers, Ticket, Pause. In the kitchen Mitch steps in from the left,
standing low enough that the wheel never covers his face; at the counter he stands behind it
on the right.

**The ticket wheel** (changed 2026-11-07 after the first look): a diner order wheel hung flat
from the ceiling, seen from the front, in the kitchen's top-left corner and partly off the
screen (the rod, the far side and the left of the ring are out of view). The order hangs
from the front clip, square on and readable; the other clips are empty. It turns left to
right: a new order comes round from the left as it is read out, and Ticket turns it once all
the way round. It is drawn in code (`js/stage.js`), not painted, so the words stay sharp at
every text size; the ticket gets wider with Large and Extra Large text. The counter has no
wheel: there, Ticket is the order's slip on the panel (it came down with the plate).

## Voices and the script

- **Voice Studio:** the new version (Bryan's `brosenberg99/voice-studio`, `main` at
  9ef89d5, 2026-11-02) replaced the copy at `F:\PROJECT FILES\TOOLS\voice-studio-main` on
  2026-11-07; the old copy is beside it as `voice-studio-main.backup-20261107`. That folder
  is now a clone of Bryan's repo, so `git pull` there brings in his updates.
- **Every character gets its own voice,** made in Voice Studio from a description (Describe a
  voice). The voices need to be hilarious: adult jokes on the customers, no baby talk.
- **One script per pack** (`packs/diner/script.js`): every line in the game (orders,
  reactions, complaints, Mitch's hints, tutorial lines, gross-out jokes), with several
  variants for each moment so it does not get repetitive. A line is `{ id, who, moment,
  text }`; the game picks among a moment's variants and never repeats one in a shift.
- **Orders are spoken from parts** in the customer's own voice ("Bacon cheeseburger" ·
  "medium" · "no onions"), since every order is different. Complaints are written per
  ingredient ("I said no cheese!"), so each is a whole recorded line.
- **Recorded lines** are bundled files listed in `packs/diner/voice/manifest.js` with a hash of
  the text they were recorded from. **Until a line is recorded, or after its text is edited,
  it falls back to text to speech**, so the script can be tweaked at any time and only the
  changed lines need re-recording. An order with any part unrecorded is spoken entirely by
  text to speech, so one sentence never mixes two voices.
- **Character Voices** setting: Voices and Captions (default) / Captions Only / Off. Scan
  labels always follow Text to Speech.

### Voice quality test

Recording stopped on 2026-11-07 at 510 of 708 lines (all 110 of Mitch's; most of each
customer's) so the voices can be checked before the rest are made. `tools/voice-test.py` makes
one line per character in twelve versions: the take in the game, two more takes, more render
steps, guidance 3.0 and 1.5, a take that carries on from the voice's own sample, two delivery
experiments per character (other delivery notes, "hun" for "hon", a [laughing] or [sigh] tag
left out), and the game's take slowed, made clearer or pitched. They are on a listening page,
`voice-source/quality-test/index.html` (open it from the folder), with thumbs up and down, a
star for the best and notes per character; "Save my picks" downloads
`burger-bar-voice-picks.json`. The picks decide the recording settings and script changes
before the remaining lines are recorded (and whether the recorded ones are redone).

**Round 1 picks (2026-11-08,** `voice-source/burger-bar-voice-picks.json`; the page and clips
moved to `voice-source/quality-test/round1/`):

| Voice | Verdict | What changes |
|---|---|---|
| Mitch | Approved; best: another take (seed 2, 176 Hz); also liked: closest to the sample, clearer; down: the game's take (232 Hz) and "Higher" | His voice stays; the intro line is now the starred take. His own voice clip is 179 Hz, but his recorded lines' median is 210 Hz: the 33 more than 25% above it (the range thumbed down) are listed in `voice-source/takes/mitch/replaced-2026-11-08/replaced.json`, to record again with the pitch-aware recorder in the final voice pass; his 11 newest lines play as text to speech until then |
| Marge | Every version down: "Deliver on hon with comma sounds unnatural. it's still distorted. let's reprompt with some different styles to choose from." | New voices in four styles; the model is given "hun" with no comma before it (captions keep "hon") |
| Gus | Every version down: "needs more western drawl and slightly faster cadence" | New voices with a Western, Southern, Texas or country drawl, not slow |
| Coach Bev | Every version down: "too high pitch. distorted. needs a butch female sounding character." | New low, butch voices. Her lines reach the voice model calm (full stops for "!", no capitals: `calm` in `tools/voices.json`; captions unchanged) and her delivery notes ask for firm, not loud: with "!" every clone shouted up to 220-360 Hz |
| Vernon | Every version down: "a foppish fem male voice similar to Frasier or his brother Niles" | New foppish, fussy, refined voices (described in words; no actor's voice is copied); his delivery notes lose "droning" |

**The distortion is measurable.** Voice Studio's clarity score (SQUIM, a PESQ estimate: about 1
distorted, 4.5 clean) averaged 3.7 for Mitch's approved voice and 2.0 to 2.3 for the others in
round 1 (Coach Bev 2.0, Marge 2.1); the designs had asked for rasp, which came out as
distortion. Round 2's descriptions avoid rasp and gravel words, and candidates are ranked by
it (`clarity()` in `tools/voices.py`).

**Round 2** (`tools/voice-styles.py` with `tools/voice-styles.json`): for Marge, Gus, Coach Bev,
Vernon and Sal, four styles each, every style designed with four seeds (six for Bev and Sal); the
best two of each style (heard right, a pitch near the character's, then the clearest) say the
character's test line cloned from that design, as the game records lines, with no delivery note,
the take kept within 15% of the voice's own pitch where one is. The first wording for Bev and Sal
asked for loudness ("loud", "barks orders", "loud-mouthed") and the model shouted, 200 to 390 Hz
for voices meant to be low; they were redone asking for low-pitched voices that speak steadily. Same page
address, `voice-source/quality-test/index.html`; each row also plays the sample the voice was
designed from; "Save my picks" downloads `burger-bar-voice-picks-2.json`. Candidates are in
`voice-source/design2/<character>/` with a report of every one made. The starred voice becomes
the character's Voice Studio voice and their lines are recorded again.

**Recording waits for the final pass.** The Food Court proposal (2026-11-08, build order 1)
plays the whole script through text to speech while the restaurants are built and records
voices last, once the script is settled; the Burger Bar's remaining lines wait too. The
round-2 picks can come any time; nothing more is recorded until the final pass. (A Mitch
re-record started that day was stopped at its first clips and undone.)

## Controls and scanning

### Contexts (ACCESSIBILITY §4)

| Context | What is on screen | Classification |
|---|---|---|
| card | Main menu, Settings, Pause, tutorial offer, results, confirmations | CHOICE |
| line | One step of the line: its answers on real objects, Ticket, Pause | CHOICE; Timed cooking is a CHOICE scan over a slowly changing world |
| playout | A short animation of a choice already applied: a patty flipping, an ingredient dropping, a customer reacting | Not scanned; then the scan resumes |

All the rules of §4 apply: every root opens on the blank -1, rows are entered with
`enterGroup(children, {wrap:true, backStop:true})` and have no Back buttons, choices act on
release, and Parking, Loops, Space Brake and Wait for Speech live only in Hub Settings.

- **Stable ids:** `add`, `skip`, `swap`, `cook:<stage>`, `takeOff`, `pick:<value>`, `serve`,
  `redo:<item>:<phase>`, `ticket`, `pause`. A redraw keeps the highlight on the same id; if
  the id is gone, the scan clears to -1.
- **Scenes breathe.** The customer's order, a reaction and a review are said in full before
  the next screen takes over (with a cap, so a stuck speech engine never freezes play).
- **Pause** is the last item on every line screen and on every card that is not itself
  Pause. Holding Enter for 5 seconds also opens it (a ring shows from 2 seconds). Pause holds
  the standard items (§7): Continue · Restart Shift · Settings · Main Menu · Exit Game.
  Restart Shift, Main Menu and Exit Game each ask first, with Cancel first.
- **Mouse and touch** work everywhere, in parallel.

### How many presses

| Order | Choices (Simple) |
|---|---|
| Hamburger or cheeseburger, as written | Grill 3 (Add, Skip, Medium) + Board 9 + Serve 1 + Next Customer 1 = 14 |
| Double cheeseburger | Grill 4 + Board 9 + Serve 1 + Next Customer 1 = 15 |

The order screen needs no press: the line starts when the customer has finished speaking.

With the highlight staying on the last answer, a run of Adds costs one Enter each with two
switches. The M1 numbers for the same cheeseburger were 29 selects and about 85 Space moves.
The playtest judges the real numbers.

## Settings

In the canonical order (ACCESSIBILITY §7), from the main menu and from Pause. Labels say what
changes, not who it is for.

| # | Setting | Values (default first) |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Text to Speech | On / Off |
| 2 | Voice | cycles available voices |
| 3 | Cooking | Simple / Timed |
| 4 | Cooking Speed | Very Slow / Slow / Normal (disabled with Simple) |
| 5 | Shift Length | 5 / 3 / 8 customers |
| 6 | Orders | Usual / Simple / Fancy |
| 7 | Assistant Manager | Hints and Jokes / Hints Only / Off |
| 8 | Character Voices | Voices and Captions / Captions Only / Off |
| 9 | Colour Profile | Standard / High Contrast |
| 10 | Motion | Full / Reduced |
| 11 | Text Size | Normal / Large / Extra Large |
| 12 | Graphics Quality | Use Hub Setting / High / Standard / Low |
| 13 | Auto Scan | Off — Two Switches / On — One Switch |
| 14 | Scan Speed | 1 s to 5 s; default 2 s |
| 15 | Sound Effects | On / Off |
| 16 | Music | On / Off |
| 17 | Reset Progress | two-step confirm |
| 18 | Back | |

Gone with the redesign: Pouring, Burning, Customers at Once, Mistake Warnings and Next-Step
Star ([What the redesign changed](#what-the-redesign-changed)). Text to Speech, Voice, Auto
Scan and Scan Speed stay even though the Hub has them, because the game is also played
outside the Hub.

## Recipes

Starting prices are in coins, tuned in M6. "Starter" means unlocked from the first shift.

| Recipe | Stations | Unlock | Price | Milestone |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hamburger | Grill, Board | Starter | 6 | M2 |
| Cheeseburger | Grill, Board | Starter | 7 | M2 |
| Bacon Cheeseburger | Grill, Board | 40 | 9 | M2 |
| Double Cheeseburger | Grill, Board | 60 | 10 | M2 |
| Fries | Fryer | Starter | 3 | M4 |
| Fountain drink (cola, lemon-lime, orange) | Drinks | Starter | 2 | M4 |
| Onion Rings | Fryer | 40 | 4 | M4 |
| Lemonade and iced tea | Drinks | 30 | 3 | M4 |
| Milkshake (chocolate, vanilla, strawberry) | Shake Bar | 60 | 5 | M5 |
| Grilled Cheese | Griddle | 50 | 5 | M5 |
| Pancake Stack | Griddle | 80 | 6 | M5 |
| Breakfast Plate (pancakes + bacon) | Griddle | 100 | 9 | M5 |
| Root Beer Float | Drinks, Shake Bar | 80 | 5 | M5 |
| More shake flavours (banana, cookies and cream, mint chip) | Shake Bar | 50 each | 5 | M5 |
| The Diner Tower (triple, bacon, everything) | Grill, Board | 150 | 14 | M2 |

Until progression arrives (M6), every recipe of the milestone in play is unlocked. An order is
one to three items, to match the Orders setting: Simple is one burger with up to two changes
from the recipe; Usual is one or two items; Fancy changes more.

## Saves

Saved to `localStorage` as things happen.

| Key | Holds |
|---|---|
| `bb-settings` | the settings above (version 2; version 1 saves are upgraded and the removed settings dropped) |
| `bb-progress:diner` | coins, unlocks, best orders, whether the tutorial has been offered |
| `bb-shift:diner` | the shift in progress, so it can be continued |

Each holds `{v, pack, packVersion, …}`. A save from a newer version is never overwritten. If
the pack has changed since the save, only the shift in progress is dropped.

## How it is built

### Built into the engine, not the burger

The game stays modular: the diner could become a pizza place, or anything else, by swapping
the content pack (`tests/packs/test-pizza` proves it). **The shared engine** holds the
assembly-line phases and their three step kinds, the five-choice limit, Add/Skip/Swap It,
the review with redo, both cooking modes with the automatic flip, accuracy scoring, the
tutorial and its warnings, gross-outs with their cues and Mitch's hints, the expression
images, the script and the voices. **A pack only supplies** its ingredients, recipes, stations
(as lists of steps), stages, gross-outs, characters, backdrops and lines, and gets every
feature for free.

### Three layers

1. **Rules, in `js/core/`.** No DOM, no THREE, no timers, seeded random numbers; every module
   also loads under Node, so every rule is tested without a browser. Kept from M0: `rng`,
   `orders`, `progress`, `savefmt`, and the ticket wording in `words`. New: `line` (the phases
   and steps of one order, the review and redo, Timed cooking, the tutorial's warnings),
   `judge` (mistakes, stars, tips), `shift` (who comes in, where gross-outs fall, the
   tutorial's order), `script` (picking lines, recordings that match their words) and
   `schema`. The free-roam modules (`items`, `kitchen`, `verbs`, `menus`, `planner`,
   `scoring` and the old `shift`) stay in the M1 archive.
2. **The diner, as data:** `packs/diner/pack.js` (ingredients, stations, recipes, cast,
   gross-outs), `packs/diner/script.js` (every line), `packs/diner/art/` and
   `packs/diner/voice/`.
3. **Presentation, in `js/`:** the 2D stage (backdrops, characters, real-object controls),
   the 3D food (Three.js, cel-shaded), cards, audio and voice, and switch input through the
   Hub's shared choice scanner (`NarbeChoiceScanAdapter`).

### Graphics quality

Follows `GRAPHICS-QUALITY.md` (hub root). Load `shared/platform.js`, then
`shared/graphics-quality.js`, before any renderer code; open the game with the stable id
`bensfoodcourt` (was `bensburgerbar` until the 2026-11-08 rename) and wait for `ready` before
creating the renderer. The setting has the stable
id `graphics-quality`. Low turns off shadows and antialiasing and caps the renderer at
1280 × 720; food, doneness marks, characters, the scan frame and all text stay the same. Low
never changes rules, timing, input or random results.

### Offline

Every dependency is local: Three.js in `js/vendor/` with its licence, art, sound and recorded
voices. No CDN links. Text to speech uses the device's own voices; recorded voices always work
offline. When the game is later copied to the website, its offline-manifest step applies
there.

### Files

```
BENSFOODCOURT/              (BENSBURGERBAR until 2026-11-08)
  DESIGN.md                 this file: Ben's Burger Bar, the template restaurant
  BENS-FOOD-COURT           the Ben's Food Court proposal (the court, seven restaurants, build order)
  BURGER-BAR-REDESIGN       the Nov 7 call's notes
  index.html
  js/core/*.js              rules (Node-testable)
  js/*.js                   presentation
  js/vendor/                pinned Three.js and its licence
  packs/diner/pack.js       the diner's data
  packs/diner/script.js     every line, with variants
  packs/diner/art/          backdrops, characters (one folder each), props, manifest.js
  packs/diner/voice/        recorded lines and manifest.js
  packs/diner/sound/        sound effects (synthesised by tools/make-sounds.cjs)
  tests/*.test.cjs          node --test "bennyshub/apps/games/BENSFOODCOURT/tests/*.test.cjs"
  tests/packs/test-pizza/   test-only proof pack (never published)
  tools/                    art and voice build scripts
  art-source/               raw generated art, its prompts and jobs (never loaded, never published)
  voice-source/             voice candidates and takes (never loaded, never published)
```

Everything for the game lives here, in Benny's PC Hub, while it is made and tested; it goes to
the website's copy on `F:` only after it is approved. That includes the working files:
`art-source/` holds the raw generated art, the ChatGPT (Codex) prompts and jobs that made it
and the review sheets, and `voice-source/` holds the voice candidates and takes. The game never
loads either; `tools/build_art.py` makes the finished, optimised images in `packs/diner/art/`,
and `tools/voices.py` the recorded lines in `packs/diner/voice/`. Neither folder goes to the
website. Every image is checked before it goes in.

### Testing on this PC

Node only: the rules under `node --test`, the whole page under jsdom driven by Space and
Enter like a switch interface, and 3D frames drawn on the CPU for review. No browser or
Electron window is opened for testing here; the final look is checked by a person.

## Milestones

| M | What gets built | Done when |
|---|---|---|
| M2 | The guided line: rules (`line`, `judge`, `gross`, `script`, `tutorial`, `shift`) for burgers; the test pizzeria on the same engine; the 2.5D stage with first art; the script with text to speech; the tutorial | Node tests: never more than five choices on a screen, every step a single press, every recipe can be made for 3 stars, the judge counts every kind of mistake, at most two gross-outs per shift and never two on one order, tutorial warnings only in the tutorial, the pizza pack needs no engine change, a shift saves and restores exactly. A jsdom run plays a whole shift with Space and Enter, two-switch and one-switch |
| M3 | Backdrops (counter, grill, board), Mitch and the first four customers with ten expressions each, cel-shaded food, voices for those five in Voice Studio | Every line those five say is recorded or falls back cleanly; Wait for Speech waits on recorded lines. **The player tries it:** presses per order, fatigue, is it fun? |
| M4 | Fryer and drinks; Timed cooking polish; more of the cast | Second playtest |
| M5 | Shake bar, griddle, the rest of the cast, walk-in pool, more script variety | |
| M6 | My Diner (coins, unlocks, decor), Free Cook, visitors' book, naming regulars | Coins never go negative; every unlock is reachable |
| M7 | Listed in the hub, after test and approval | ACCESSIBILITY §10 checklist; the player goes hub → game → shift → pause → settings → hub alone with their own switch setup |

## What the redesign changed

How the Nov 7 call and the earlier design were reconciled. "Call" is the call's notes;
"proposal" marks a point the call left open, with a default written in for review.

| Earlier design | Now | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Free-roam stations: Go To, bins, slots, Take, Put Down, Send, holding things | The assembly line: each step offers one thing | Call |
| Flip as a choice | Automatic, part of the animation | Call |
| Pick the Doneness / Time It Yourself | Simple (default) / Timed (challenge) | Call |
| Patty stages Raw → Blue Rare → Rare → Medium → Well Done → Extra Crispy, two sides scored separately | Rare / Medium / Medium Well / Well Done to pick; Timed runs on to Charred; one doneness per patty | Call (four options); proposal (Charred, one per patty) |
| Burning setting (food can burn) | Gone: in Timed, Charred is the end and costs one mistake | Proposal |
| Stack order scored | The line's order is the stack's order | Proposal |
| Sauce amounts (dab, line, lots); Auto-Fill and Hold to Pour | Add or Skip, always a normal squeeze; the Pouring setting is gone | Call (Add or Skip) |
| Bacon cooked on the grill (chewy or crispy) | Pre-cooked bacon on the board line; charred bacon is a gross-out | Proposal |
| Order card after each serve, checks scored 0.4 to 1, stars from the average | Stars by mistakes (none, one or two, more); the customer's review names each mistake; a receipt slip lists them | Call |
| Hidden favourites add to the score | They add a bonus tip and a line, never stars | Proposal (keeps three stars obvious) |
| Mistake Warnings setting | Warnings only in the tutorial, before the mistake lands | Call |
| Next-Step Star | Gone: the line only ever asks the next thing | Call |
| Customers at Once, Tickets row, one tray per order | One customer at a time; more at once moves to Later (a day at the diner) | Call (flow) |
| Check the Tray, remaking | The review, with Redo the Grill and Redo the Toppings | Call |
| — | Tutorial | Call |
| — | Gross-outs with Swap It | Call; the spoken label is a proposal |
| 3D toon customers, `worldui` choice plates | 2D characters with about ten whole-pose images each; real-object controls | Call |
| Box-art kitchen | 2D backdrops per station, 3D food | Call |
| Walk-ins built from parts | A pool of extra drawn customers, later | Proposal |
| `content/voice-lines.json` (M4) | `packs/diner/script.js` with variants, recorded lines with text hashes, text-to-speech fallback | Call |
| Main menu: Start Shift · Free Cook · My Diner · How to Play · Settings · Exit | Start Shift · Tutorial · Settings · Exit Game; My Diner and Free Cook return in M6 | Proposal |
| Mitch speaks only into silence | Still, except a gross-out line, said as the bad ingredient arrives | Proposal |
| Website paths (`bennyshub/ACCESSIBILITY.md`, `docs/development/…`, `npm run audit:release`, `D:\Projects\Assets\`) | PC hub paths: `ACCESSIBILITY.md` and `GRAPHICS-QUALITY.md` at the hub root; working art and voices in the game's `art-source/` and `voice-source/` | This PC |

**Kept as they were:** the writing tone; the cast as drafted; Mitch, his look and his rules
for when he talks; tips; no zero and no fail; never scoring time; Pause last plus the
5-second hold; the Settings rows that wrap the Hub's managers; the save format; the
seeded, Node-tested rules and the test pizzeria; Graphics Quality; offline; an unlisted first
release; My Diner, Free Cook and naming regulars as later work.

## Later

- **A day at the diner** (Ari): from quiet hours with one customer to a rush with more, with
  no deadlines. Brings back more than one customer at once.
- **My Diner and Free Cook** (M6): recipes, decor and upgrades bought with tips (upgrades add
  room or flair, never speed); every ingredient with no tickets, and Feed Someone.
- **Naming regulars:** a walk-in served three times becomes a regular, named from
  suggestions or spelled with the Pet Pals keyboard (`NARBEPETPALS/js/game.js`).
- **Caregiver mode:** a menu editor and a favourite-customer editor, behind the spoken
  warning before any mouse-only screen (ACCESSIBILITY §7). The pack format already holds
  recipes, customers and lines as data.

## Open questions

From the Nov 7 call:

1. **Pose swap:** a quick bounce, or just a cut? *Proposal: bounce on Full motion, cut on
   Reduced.*
2. **The roughly ten expressions:** which goes with which moment? *Proposal: the
   [Expressions](#expressions-proposal-the-mapping-is-open) table.*
3. **Final menus:** 2D or 3D? *Proposal: 2D diner boards.*
4. **The cast list and each character's personality.** *Proposal: start with the cast
   drafted on 2026-11-01; first art and voices for Mitch, Marge, Gus, Coach Bev and Vernon.*

From the reconcile, for Ari, Bryan and Tabby:

5. Bacon moves to the board line (pre-cooked), so the grill is patties only and charred bacon
   is the "overcooked" gross-out. OK?
6. Four doneness options (Rare, Medium, Medium Well, Well Done), or five with Medium Rare?
7. Swap It appears only on a bad ingredient, and its spoken label names the problem ("Add the
   green cheese"), so the cue never depends on sight or on Mitch alone. OK?
8. The tutorial order includes one gross-out, to teach Swap It. OK?
9. Sauce amounts and "extra pickles" leave the tickets (Add or Skip has no amount). Coach
   Bev's favourite becomes a line about the pickles. OK?
10. Settings removed: Pouring, Burning, Customers at Once, Mistake Warnings, Next-Step Star.
    OK?
11. Walk-ins become a pool of extra drawn customers. OK?

## Decided

**Ben's Food Court (2026-11-08)**

- The game pivots to Ben's Food Court, and this restaurant is the template for the others
  (proposal: `BENS-FOOD-COURT` in this folder). The folder is `BENSFOODCOURT`, the id
  `bensfoodcourt`. Not yet built: the court, its menu and the other restaurants.

**Voice test, round 1 (2026-11-08)**

- Mitch's voice is approved; Marge, Gus, Coach Bev and Vernon get new voices
  ([Voice quality test](#voice-quality-test)): Gus with a Western drawl and a quicker pace, Bev
  low and butch, Vernon foppish and fussy, Marge in a choice of styles with a natural "hon".
- A new customer, **Sal**: a gruff, crude New Yorker who runs the hot-dog cart and never takes
  lettuce ([Customers and cast](#customers-and-cast)).

**First playtest (2026-11-07)**

- The ticket spells out every patty and everything that goes on ([The ticket](#the-ticket)).
- The ticket wheel is a front view of a flat diner order wheel in the kitchen's top-left
  corner, turning left to right; none at the counter ([No floating UI](#no-floating-ui)).
- Sauces are squirted from their squeeze bottles.
- Nothing in a backdrop is out of focus (the counter queue's blur is gone).
- Voice recording waits for the [voice quality test](#voice-quality-test).

**Nov 7 design call (Ari, Bryan, Tabby)**

- The guided, phase-based assembly line replaces the free-roam station scan: order, grill,
  board, review, serve. The ingredients come to the player.
- Every screen: -1, Pause, and no more than about five choices.
- Flipping is automatic. Cooking is Simple (default; four or five doneness options, always
  comes out as chosen) or Timed (a forgiving challenge, the one place cooking can go wrong).
- Rating by accuracy, never time: 3 stars for everything right, 2 for one or two mistakes,
  1 for more; no zero, no fail. The customer's review says exactly what was wrong.
- An optional tutorial, with warnings before a mistake lands; no warnings outside it.
- Random gross-outs, one or two per shift at most, flagged visually and by Mitch; catching
  one keeps the order clean, missing it gets a disgusted reaction.
- 2.5D: 3D food and cooking; 2D cel-shaded original characters with about ten whole-pose
  expression images, big on screen; 2D backdrops per station with hidden funny details;
  final menus may be 2D.
- No floating UI: real objects in the diner (the ticket wheel, the cash register).
- Pull the new Voice Studio and replace the old one; every character gets its own voice;
  the voices are hilarious, adult, with the jokes on the customers.
- Everything above lives in the shared engine; a pack only supplies content.
- One editable script per pack, with variety; re-record only changed lines; text to speech
  until a line is recorded.

**Ari's review (2026-11-01)**, as it stands after the redesign

1. Cooking has two modes; timed cooking is very forgiving, and everything works with single
   presses.
2. Customers at Once was approved; with one customer at a time on the line, it moves to
   Later.
3. Pause: approved.
4. Editors: room left for them; caregiver editors come later.
5. Mitch: approved; he talks while the scan is parked.

**Bryan (2026-11-01 and 11-02)**

- **Title:** Ben's Burger Bar. The folder `BENSBURGERBAR` and the id `bensburgerbar` are final.
  *(Superseded 2026-11-08: the game became Ben's Food Court, folder `BENSFOODCOURT`, id
  `bensfoodcourt`; Ben's Burger Bar is its first restaurant.)*
- **Shift Length** stays at 5 customers by default until the playtests.
- **Cast changes:** Gus orders two of one thing; Oakes sounds nasal and nerdy; Dale is the
  onion-ring guy; add Kai and Vernon; add walk-ins for variety.
- **Help without giving the answer:** reminders of what the customer wants stay on (the
  ticket is always in view and Ticket says it again); saying something is wrong does not,
  except in the tutorial.
- **Review, redo, then learn:** the player may check and redo before serving; a messed-up
  order is served as made and the review says what went wrong.

## M2 notes

Built 2026-11-07, in Benny's PC Hub only (not on the website, not in `games.json`).

- **Rules** (`js/core/`): `line` (phases, the three step kinds, the review and redo, Timed
  cooking's clock), `judge`, `shift` (who comes in, where gross-outs fall), `script`, `words`,
  `schema`, plus Bryan's `orders`, `rng`, `progress` and `savefmt` (settings version 2). The
  test pizzeria runs on the same engine. `node --test "bennyshub/apps/games/BENSFOODCOURT/tests/*.test.cjs"`
  (Node 22 wants the pattern, not the folder) runs 53 tests, among them 600 bot shifts across
  every setting, 600 seeds of gross-out placement, and 120 shifts checking Sal never gets lettuce. Press counts measured by the tests: 13 line choices for a hamburger or
  cheeseburger, 14 for a double or the Tower (plus Next Customer).
- **The page** (`js/`): `stage` (2D layers and real-object controls), `world` (3D food,
  cel-shaded; the game still plays if WebGL will not start), `ui` (the hub's choice scanner,
  keys, menu boards), `game` (the flow), `audio` (sound effects and voices), `save`.
- **Played through the real page** with the hub's real scanner, by Space and Enter only, in
  jsdom (`tests/dom-play.cjs`): the tutorial (every deliberate mistake was warned, none
  landed), shifts with two switches, one switch (Auto Scan), Timed cooking, Fancy orders with
  captions only and Mitch off, Pause, Settings, Main Menu and Continue Shift, and an
  eight-customer shift that serves the whole cast (it fails if an order leaves off a customer's
  dislike and their `order.dislike` line is not said).
- **Art:** made with ChatGPT through Codex (`art-source/jobs/`, prompts by
  `write_prompts.py`); cut out with BiRefNet and exported by `tools/build_art.py`. Review
  frames without a browser: `tools/review-frames.cjs` draws the real 3D scene on the CPU and
  `tools/review_compose.py` lays the 2D stage over it (`art-source/review/frames/`). Sal was
  made the same way on 2026-11-08 (`write_prompts.py models sal`, then `poses sal`; sheet
  `art-source/review/sal-poses.png`); `node tools/review-frames.cjs <out> sal` shoots only his
  session (ordering, the lettuce line, the reaction).
- **Voices:** designed in Voice Studio from `tools/voices.json` (`tools/voices.py design`):
  the picks are in Voice Studio's library as "Burger Bar: Mitch", "…: Marge", "…: Gus",
  "…: Coach Bev" and "…: Vernon", and every candidate is in `voice-source/design/`. Lines are
  recorded with `node tools/voice-clips.cjs voice-source/clips.json` then
  `tools/voices.py record voice-source/clips.json` (708 clips: the script's lines and every
  order part for each customer); a clip whose words change is recorded again and plays as
  text to speech until then. Stopped at 510 for the [voice quality test](#voice-quality-test):
  do not record the rest until the picks are in. Round 2's new voices come from
  `tools/voice-styles.py`; with Sal the script needs 852 line clips plus 57 order parts per customer
  (1042 clips in all). Since 2026-11-08 the recorder gives the voice model each character's
  respellings (`respell` in `tools/voices.json`: Marge's "hon" becomes "hun"), and keeps a take only
  near the voice's own pitch (15%, 25% with a delivery note) and clear, else the nearest of four;
  `voices.py use <character> <candidate>` makes a round-2 pick the character's voice and takes
  their old recordings out of the manifest.
- **Sounds** are synthesised by `tools/make-sounds.cjs` (soft and short for anything heard
  every step); `node tools/make-sounds.cjs squirt` writes just the named ones.
- **Motion:** `tests/dom-play.cjs` has a Motion Full shift that checks the wheel turns and
  stops square on, the bottles squirt, and Ticket works in the kitchen and at the counter. The
  review frames include the wheel mid-turn and the bottle mid-squirt.
- **Not verified here:** how it looks and runs in a real browser (browsers are not opened for
  testing on this PC; the review frames are an approximation), the voices by ear, and the
  timing of real speech. The player's playtest is the check.

## History

**M0 (2026-11-02, Bryan).** The rules engine: seeded, Node-tested, no ingredient names in the
rules, one stage process for cooking, a test pizzeria that needed no rules changes, saves
that restore exactly. Its order generator, words, progress and save format carry into M2;
its kitchen, verbs, menu builder and planner bot were the free-roam flow.

**M1 (2026-11-02, Bryan).** The free-roam greybox: grill, board and counter with the scan on
the kitchen itself. Measured in the browser: 54 selects and 150 Space moves for a two-burger
order, 28 selects for a plain hamburger. Those numbers led to the Nov 7 redesign. The build
is archived at `archives/games/2026-11-07/BENSBURGERBAR-M1` (Bryan's `burger-bar` branch,
4c12eff3).
